{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792336828,"nonce":"oS/aaF8s3LsNxwRT81MccODNM9g=","message":"Revised plan, 2026-10-18, after the user's answers and a read of krio, Ash and Zyntax. This supersedes \"Which interpreter\", \"The language\" and the milestones above; the host design (op stream, capabilities, contributions, settings registry) stands.\n\n- The language is RayScript: hscript's logic (Haxe expression syntax, dynamic) and HXX markup in one language, with ashui's APIs and the editor's APIs for presenting views and for logic, able to bind native FFIs.\n- A script runs in fibers; krio and how Ash uses fibers for its threads are the model.\n- Zyntax is a candidate: its host-driven fibers, the Python and Lua frontends, and zynml's use of the Zyn grammar were to be examined.\n\nAsh and krio:\n- Ash runs Haxe thread bodies on krio fibers (ash_interp mod.rs:2720). Its interpreter and both JITs poll at loop back-edges: they compare one global epoch word, which a 2 ms timer thread bumps, and call hlp_fiber_poll only when it changed (ash std fiber.rs:77-98; cranelift backend.rs:545). A native call that blocks holds up every fiber on its scheduler thread (ash docs/hdll.md:56); Ash has a worker lane for that.\n- krio-fiber is stackful, !Send, with cancel, deadline and panic capture. krio-core's Task (resume gives Yielded, Pending or Done) is what krio-runtime's RoundRobin, krio-preempt's TimeSliceScheduler and krio-parallel's cluster drive; krio-parallel only takes Send tasks, and its waker registry parks a Pending task until woken.\n\nZyntax fibers, host-driven (the `fiber def` machines):\n- Shipped: a host creates, resumes, cancels and frees fibers; one thread drives many (tiered.rs:2044-2180; fiber.rs:94-137).\n- Not there: the bytecode interpreter refuses fibers (\"this function needs a JIT tier\", hir_interp.rs:2577), so they exist only on a Cranelift build. No time budget or fuel; a script that never yields blocks the host thread, and cancel is seen only at the next resume. Fibers take no arguments, yield and resume with a 62-bit integer, and the default stack is 64 KiB the host cannot change. Host calls are synchronous; a call that suspends the fiber until the host answers is not shipped (the zylua yield-then-take-input pattern could do it, unconfirmed). No runtime source spans. The embedder, handler stack and fiber tables are per-process or per-thread singletons. Hot reload of a live fiber goes through loop-boundary OSR only.\n\nZyntax frontends and the Zyn grammar:\n- Python and Lua do not use the Zyn grammar: ruff and full_moon parse, then scope, whole-program type inference and a hand-written lowering (13k to 17k lines each) to typed AST. Dynamic values are boxed `Any`; both have a library of dynamic operators and a snapshot of it built at compile time (zyntax_builtins, zb_any_*). Neither has a sandbox: io and os are always linked. Startup is about 28 to 35 ms for a small program; the LLVM build is about 100 MB.\n- The Zyn engine is a packrat interpreter with a compiled parsing machine, and machine compile is under a millisecond. For a RayScript grammar it lacks: a configurable whitespace and comment policy (skip_ws is fixed), a predicate to match a closing tag to its opening one, block-bodied lambdas and mutable capture (zynml's own capture test is ignored), map and object literals, general string interpolation, working source spans (`.span` returns 0,0), and error recovery or incremental parsing. A JSX-like island is possible in principle (the f-string rule re-enters the expression grammar from a hole), but a statement starting with `\u003c` is read as a comparison, so it needs a leading keyword.\n\nBuild RayScript's own front end and bytecode VM in Rust, with fibers as krio Tasks, and keep Zyntax as a later second backend rather than the first runtime. Why:\n- The needs here are time slicing under a frame budget, fibers the host steps and cancels, a host call that suspends until answered, hot reload at a yield, a capability sandbox, exact error spans and an error-tolerant parser for the editor. Zyntax has none of these on its fiber path today, and each is upstream work; Ash's and krio's pieces already have the shape.\n- A VM fiber is heap data (frames and an operand stack), not a native stack. It implements krio's Task, so RoundRobin and TimeSliceScheduler drive it; it is Send, so krio-parallel can place it; a budget is an epoch and fuel check at back-edges and calls, as Ash does; cancel is a flag read at each poll, so an infinite loop is stoppable; hot reload is a function-table swap at a yield with a layout check; a line table gives spans and stack traces.\n- Extension scripts are glue and views, not number crunching; JIT speed is not the need, and a tree-walking hscript is the bar to beat.\n- A hand-written Rust parser (Pratt expressions, markup, error recovery, exact spans) is what the Python and Lua crates did and what editor tooling needs.\nWhat Zyntax stays good for: a second backend (RayScript AST to typed AST to HIR) for hot functions; its snapshot and host-module descriptions as models; ZRTL for native libraries; the Zyn grammar for smaller DSLs. What would move this: Zyntax gaining interpreter-tier fibers, a fuel hook and a host-answered suspend.\n\n- Syntax: hscript's (var, function, lambdas `(a) -\u003e e`, blocks, if, for in, while, switch, try, anonymous objects, arrays, maps, string interpolation, `new`, metadata, optional type annotations) and HXX markup as expressions with `{expr}` holes and `\u003cif\u003e`/`\u003cfor\u003e`; imports; `await`; native declarations (below). hscript's own scripts should parse unchanged. Its standard library (Std, Math, Array, String, Map, StringTools, Lambda, Reflect, Json) is reimplemented natively, not Haxe's.\n- Views: the reactive core (signal, computed, effect) runs in the VM and emits the op stream; ashui's tags and props are the vocabulary. Logic: editor APIs as host modules (workspace, buffers, commands, status items, settings, key bindings, tasks, terminal, diagnostics), called as async host calls that park the fiber.\n- One API description, generated from the Haxe sources by a macro (ashui and RayDE annotated), feeds the VM's host modules and RayDE's own completion, hover and diagnostics for RayScript, so docs and tooling cannot drift from the runtime.\n- Fibers: an extension has a supervisor fiber; each event handler, effect rerun and `spawn(...)` is a fiber; structured cancellation. The host scheduler gives each tick a budget (about 2 ms across extensions, as Ash's timer does), per-extension fuel is metered, and an extension over budget is throttled and flagged on the Extensions page.\n- Native FFI: declarations in RayScript (library name, functions, struct layouts, callbacks) bound with a C-ABI caller. Native code cannot be sandboxed and a native fault takes the process down (f775096 showed a native panic aborts it), so: a capability per library, shown on the Extensions page with its path and hash; calls marked `blocking` run on a worker lane with the fiber Pending, as Ash does, so one slow call does not stall the others; and the extension host is a separate process (like CompilerServer), not a thread. Native calls into script (callbacks) run on the VM thread as new fibers; they do not yield across the native frame.\n\nM0, one week, two spikes. (a) RayScript in Rust: a hand-written parser with markup and holes and error recovery, a bytecode VM with a Task fiber, the epoch poll and a host call that parks, running a counter component to ops; measure cold start, ops per frame and memory per fiber. (b) Zyntax, two days: a minimal frontend path on a Cranelift-only build, to measure binary size, first-call compile latency and fiber creation, and to confirm the gaps above. Exit: numbers and a decision.\nM1: host side with no VM, as before (op protocol, renderer, tag registry, run-time Tw), driven by a JSON file of ops.\nM2: front end, VM, fibers, reactive core, hscript std, spans, `rayscript check`.\nM3: the API description from Haxe; host modules for ashui and the editor; manifest and contributions; the Extensions page.\nM4: native FFI and process isolation.\nM5: RayScript support in RayDE (completion, diagnostics, hover), hot reload, packaged bytecode; then a Zyntax backend for hot code if it is wanted.\n\n1. Is the recommendation right: own front end and VM first, Zyntax as a later backend? If Zyntax first, the upstream work is the list under \"Not there\" above.\n2. The extension host as a separate process (recommended, because of FFI and native aborts)?\n3. FFI trust: a prompt for each library, or only libraries a workspace has been trusted with?\n4. Type annotations: parse and use them for tooling and completion only, not enforced at run time (recommended), or checked when a script loads?\n5. A file extension (.rays?).","files":null}]}